00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:54.020
Throughout this tutorial, you will notice
that apart from compose and proto data store,

00:02:54.020 --> 00:03:00.090
I use almost no libraries from Android jetpack
In fact, very few third party libraries in

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:06.730
general, by relying on kotlin and Java standard
libraries and the Android SDK, my code becomes

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:10.970
more resistant to deprecations and changes
in libraries.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:17.590
This is because the Android SDK, and the standard
libraries tend to change less frequently than

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:21.110
third party libraries, such as those you see
an Android jetpack.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:26.020
It also means that some things which libraries
like jetpack viewmodel, jetpack, navigation

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:29.590
or help might handle must be hand written
by us.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:33.250
I actually like that, but you might have a
different value system.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:37.610
And my goal here is not to discourage you
from learning these tools if you're interested

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:38.610
in them.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:43.960
With that being said, you might be surprised
at how easy it is to write your own viewmodel

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:49.842
navigation or dependency injection code without
them in a small app like this one.

00:03:49.842 --> 00:03:54.780
This application uses Model View whatever
architecture, which is my way of saying that

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.790
I don't follow anyone else's style.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:02.210
Having studied this topic for many years,
I let the project requirements in my understanding

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:06.770
of the principles of good software design
guide my architecture.

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:12.240
In this case, I find that compose is made
for an MVVM based approach, but I added a

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:15.820
presentation logic class for a specific reason.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.720
This reason is called passive view or humble
object.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:24.820
Instead of having the view or the view model
manage the control flow of each screen, I

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.680
pulled that logic into a separate class.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.950
This class is super easy to write and test
because it has no third party dependencies.

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:36.920
And it prevents my view model from becoming
an ugly God object.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.440
You should try it sometime.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.430
I designed this architecture simply by applying
the single most important principle of software

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:47.400
architecture, separation of concerns.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.000
That's it for this part of the series.

