00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:43.340
Anyways, most of the code in this package
is simple, but it does include a design pattern,

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.650
which I will introduce now.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:51.980
This package contains several interfaces,
which are used to employ the repository design

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:52.980
pattern.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.720
This pattern is also known as the facade pattern.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:01.030
And the general goal of the pattern is simpler
than its technical definition.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:06.160
The technical definition of the facade or
repository pattern is to hide the details

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:12.400
of a subsystem, in this case, data storage
mechanisms behind an abstraction, in this

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.200
case, an interface.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.720
Let's look at a practical example.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:21.690
To make sense out of that definition, our
presentation logic classes will hold references

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:28.340
to these repository interfaces, instead of
the classes which implement the interfaces.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.040
This gives several benefits to our presentation
logic classes.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:37.320
They can be built independently of each other
without causing a compiler error, they can

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:43.210
be tested with a fake implementation of the
interface without requiring us to change any

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:47.010
code within the presentation logic class.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:53.470
Also, if we decide to use a different implementation
of the interface, such as switching from file

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:59.230
storage to a room database, we can also do
that without requiring any changes to the

00:06:59.230 --> 00:07:01.610
presentation logic class.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:06.360
These benefits are a result of building software
systems, which are loosely coupled.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:12.070
And the repository or facade pattern is an
easy way to promote loose coupling.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:16.370
In fact, interfaces in general tend to promote
loose coupling.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.220
Now don't feel the need to use this pattern
everywhere.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:25.140
A good general rule is to use them in significant
architectural boundaries.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:29.990
In this case, I'm using them as a boundary
between the front end and the back end of

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:31.650
this application.

