00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:25.160
Okay, so let me start by explaining what exactly
a hash code or hash value is.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:31.110
So it's essentially some kind of generated
key or unique identifier based on some kind

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:32.269
of algorithm.

00:16:32.269 --> 00:16:38.529
In this case, I have a very simple algorithm,
all I do is multiply the x value by 100.

00:16:38.529 --> 00:16:40.589
And I leave the y value alone.

00:16:40.589 --> 00:16:45.740
And I basically just combine those two values
together into an integer.

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:52.720
Now, the reason why I multiply x by 100, is
if I didn't do that in a nine by nine Sudoku

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:57.890
puzzle, there would be certain edge cases
where the even though the X and Y value are

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:04.860
technically different, the resulting hash
code would not be unique for several different

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:07.209
nodes.

00:17:07.209 --> 00:17:12.649
Simply put, I'm multiplying by 100 to make
the hash codes unique for every individual

00:17:12.649 --> 00:17:15.059
tile in the Sudoku puzzle.

00:17:15.059 --> 00:17:19.439
As for why we're using hash code, in general,
I'll try and keep it fairly straightforward.

00:17:19.439 --> 00:17:25.929
Basically, we're going to be storing each
node in the graph in a linked hash map.

00:17:25.929 --> 00:17:29.390
So the hash values will represent the key
for that hash map.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:34.750
A hash map has key value pairs, in case you're
not aware it, we'll be seeing that in a moment.

00:17:34.750 --> 00:17:39.789
But this turns out to be really useful because
our user interface as well will be representing

00:17:39.789 --> 00:17:43.480
things in that kind of X and Y coordinate
style.

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:44.580
So just take my word for it.

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:48.369
Hash codes are pretty handy in situations
where you have a large number of elements,

00:17:48.369 --> 00:17:53.600
and you don't necessarily want to have to
maintain a reference for every individual

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:54.600
element.

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:57.679
Instead, we can just retrieve a reference
by getting a hash code.

