01:52:14.949 --> 01:52:17.329
for the act of game feature.

01:52:17.329 --> 01:52:22.559
Before we do that, let us look at a few design
decisions involved in this architecture.

01:52:22.559 --> 01:52:27.340
The purpose of our presentation logic class,
which I call logic, for short, is exactly

01:52:27.340 --> 01:52:32.780
as the name implies, it handles the work of
coordinating the container view model and

01:52:32.780 --> 01:52:34.669
backend repositories.

01:52:34.669 --> 01:52:40.110
If notified of a non stop event, it will also
cancel all co routines, it does not possess

01:52:40.110 --> 01:52:45.559
any Android platform code, which makes it
loosely coupled and very easy to test.

01:52:45.559 --> 01:52:49.610
I might also consider reusing it for a desktop
version of this app.

01:52:49.610 --> 01:52:50.760
But we'll see.

01:52:50.760 --> 01:52:56.130
The purpose of the view model is also to do
exactly what the name implies, it is a virtual

01:52:56.130 --> 01:53:00.630
representation of the user interface, which
the view observes.

01:53:00.630 --> 01:53:06.409
In simpler terms, it is a model of the view,
it exposes function types, which is a very

01:53:06.409 --> 01:53:10.150
simple and easy standing for the observer
pattern.

01:53:10.150 --> 01:53:14.119
In situations where we don't require multiple
observers.

01:53:14.119 --> 01:53:19.209
Each time our logic class updates the view
model, the view model will automatically publish

01:53:19.209 --> 01:53:21.679
the new data to the view.

01:53:21.679 --> 01:53:27.580
Another design decision with this view model
is that it does not extend jetpack view model.

01:53:27.580 --> 01:53:31.810
There are several reasons for this decision,
some of them simple, and some of them quite

01:53:31.810 --> 01:53:32.810
technical.

01:53:32.810 --> 01:53:37.070
The simple reason is that using jetpack view
model creates tight coupling with the Android

01:53:37.070 --> 01:53:38.239
platform.

01:53:38.239 --> 01:53:42.630
And it has its own set of boilerplate code
and dependencies, which I'm not a huge fan

01:53:42.630 --> 01:53:43.630
of.

01:53:43.630 --> 01:53:48.659
In short, it doesn't solve more problems than
it creates in this particular application.

01:53:48.659 --> 01:53:53.590
And I wanted to practice creating view models
which might be usable for kotlin desktop or

01:53:53.590 --> 01:53:54.590
kotlin.

01:53:54.590 --> 01:53:55.590
j s.

01:53:55.590 --> 01:54:01.400
The technical reason why is that in this application,
we simply don't need to persist the data across

01:54:01.400 --> 01:54:06.719
activity instances or process death in order
to have a good user experience.

01:54:06.719 --> 01:54:11.289
Instead, we just make a fairly cheap call
to the Android file system and reload the

01:54:11.289 --> 01:54:13.690
data from there if such events occur.

01:54:13.690 --> 01:54:18.929
Now, before you apply that reasoning in every
situation, understand that reloading data

01:54:18.929 --> 01:54:25.019
from a file system works fine in this application,
but should not be considered a suitable replacement

01:54:25.019 --> 01:54:29.510
for unsaved instance state in every application
you write.

01:54:29.510 --> 01:54:33.230
If you like the models in save state handle,
go right ahead and use it.

01:54:33.230 --> 01:54:38.800
We also employ the strategy pattern to clean
up the interface which our logic class exposes

01:54:38.800 --> 01:54:40.880
to the container in the view.

01:54:40.880 --> 01:54:47.310
Each subclass of the sealed class represents
an action that can occur in the view or container.

01:54:47.310 --> 01:54:50.260
Rather than having a function for every UI
event.

01:54:50.260 --> 01:54:55.429
We have one function that accepts a single
object that can represent multiple different

01:54:55.429 --> 01:54:57.520
paths of execution.

01:54:57.520 --> 01:54:59.739
That's the strategy pattern.

01:54:59.739 --> 01:55:07.619
Right click On the UI package, and go to new
package called active game, right click on

01:55:07.619 --> 01:55:13.820
this new package, go to New kotlin file or
class, and we're going to create an interface,

01:55:13.820 --> 01:55:19.280
and it's going to be called active game container.

01:55:19.280 --> 01:55:21.670
This word container is a technical term.

01:55:21.670 --> 01:55:26.320
The way I'm using it here is to kind of signify
something which contains a large portion of

01:55:26.320 --> 01:55:29.020
an application or an entire application.

01:55:29.020 --> 01:55:33.449
In my opinion, a container doesn't usually
deal much with the business kind of logic

01:55:33.449 --> 01:55:34.449
of the application.

01:55:34.449 --> 01:55:39.699
It basically just wires things together and
builds things and kind of serves as an entry

01:55:39.699 --> 01:55:40.760
point.

01:55:40.760 --> 01:55:45.320
In the next part of this tutorial, I'll explain
what we'll actually be using as a container.

01:55:45.320 --> 01:55:49.219
But by using an interface here, I'm basically
stating quite explicitly that I might change

01:55:49.219 --> 01:55:52.090
my mind about what we use as a container.

01:55:52.090 --> 01:56:05.419
Anyways, it only contains two abstract functions.

