03:16:45.600 --> 03:16:49.601
Let's start by asking a question, what are
graph data structures?

03:16:49.601 --> 03:16:54.410
The simplest verbal explanation I can think
of is that they are a family of data structures,

03:16:54.410 --> 03:16:59.410
which are useful for modeling a bunch of things
which are connected in some way.

03:16:59.410 --> 03:17:03.740
To connect that definition with something
visual, you can imagine that the bunch of

03:17:03.740 --> 03:17:09.010
things are a series of nodes or points, and
the connections between them are a series

03:17:09.010 --> 03:17:13.250
of edges, which you can think of as lines
connecting the points.

03:17:13.250 --> 03:17:18.021
So whenever I say the phrase graph data structure,
a better term for most people to understand

03:17:18.021 --> 03:17:21.511
this topic is a network data structure.

03:17:21.511 --> 03:17:26.390
In fact, that is exactly how they are used
in apps like Facebook to represent users and

03:17:26.390 --> 03:17:27.390
friends.

03:17:27.390 --> 03:17:32.400
Google Maps to represent city addresses in
the roads connecting them or Sudoku puzzles

03:17:32.400 --> 03:17:35.521
to represent tiles which have relationships
to each other.

03:17:35.521 --> 03:17:37.230
According to the game's rules.

03:17:37.230 --> 03:17:40.960
Pictures and words are fine, but the code
is more important.

03:17:40.960 --> 03:17:45.620
How do we tell a computer system to virtually
represent a Sudoku puzzle?

03:17:45.620 --> 03:17:49.880
This process is more concrete than you might
think, though, it helps to be an expert in

03:17:49.880 --> 03:17:52.240
the programming language you're using.

03:17:52.240 --> 03:17:56.761
In principle, the process is to take all of
the information you know about the problem

03:17:56.761 --> 03:18:01.890
you're solving, ie the problem domain, and
start writing code which describes it.

03:18:01.890 --> 03:18:07.400
First, we represent a single tile in a Sudoku
puzzle with the class Sudoku node.

03:18:07.400 --> 03:18:13.780
It has a value x and y coordinates and a Boolean
to establish whether it can be edited or not

03:18:13.780 --> 03:18:15.330
by the user.

03:18:15.330 --> 03:18:20.380
Read Only true would indicate a node which
is a given clue at the start of a new Sudoku

03:18:20.380 --> 03:18:21.380
game.

03:18:21.380 --> 03:18:26.771
Now this term color really just means a value
from zero to the boundary of the puzzle.

03:18:26.771 --> 03:18:30.801
So a nine by nine puzzle would have the colors
zero to nine.

03:18:30.801 --> 03:18:36.381
To be honest, I don't like using the term
color as it confused my hyper literal brain.

03:18:36.381 --> 03:18:39.830
But please understand that it really is just
a number value.

03:18:39.830 --> 03:18:44.660
And the term color is actually irrelevant
in this problem domain.

03:18:44.660 --> 03:18:49.350
Where the word came from is that this kind
of data structure was used to solve a problem

03:18:49.350 --> 03:18:55.540
that involved coloring different countries
on a map using a fixed set of colors.

03:18:55.540 --> 03:18:59.891
We could have just as easily associated these
numbers with different kinds of sandwiches,

03:18:59.891 --> 03:19:02.200
but to the computer, it is the same thing.

03:19:02.200 --> 03:19:07.540
In any case, if you hear me say color, just
remember it's a value, it's a number, not

03:19:07.540 --> 03:19:09.480
literally a color.

03:19:09.480 --> 03:19:14.390
Now the data structure itself must include
every element, as well as the connections

03:19:14.390 --> 03:19:17.110
or relationships between every element.

03:19:17.110 --> 03:19:23.181
There is no single way to do this, but I arrived
at using a linked hash map datatype from the

03:19:23.181 --> 03:19:25.000
kotlin standard library.

03:19:25.000 --> 03:19:29.680
This data type preserves the ordering of its
elements, and allows me to find a specific

03:19:29.680 --> 03:19:32.390
element based on a hash code.

03:19:32.390 --> 03:19:37.280
The hash code itself comes from this simple
hashing function which generates a unique

03:19:37.280 --> 03:19:40.630
key based on the x and y values of a node.

03:19:40.630 --> 03:19:46.010
The linked lists themselves represent both
the root node at a given x&amp;y location as well

03:19:46.010 --> 03:19:52.061
as any other node which happens to be in the
same row column or sub grid of that root node.

03:19:52.061 --> 03:19:57.100
The root node will always be the first node
in the list known as the head element.

03:19:57.100 --> 03:20:02.060
But other than that, we don't care about the
ordering of the rest of the So after I figured

03:20:02.060 --> 03:20:07.240
out how to model a Sudoku game in this way,
the next step was to start writing the algorithms

03:20:07.240 --> 03:20:10.141
to manipulate my chosen data structure.

03:20:10.141 --> 03:20:13.271
The first algorithm I wrote was called build
nodes.

03:20:13.271 --> 03:20:16.931
Its job was to build the skeleton of our data
structure.

03:20:16.931 --> 03:20:20.230
By adding an element for every tile in the
puzzle.

03:20:20.230 --> 03:20:25.620
A square size in Sudoku will have n squared
tiles or elements.

03:20:25.620 --> 03:20:30.060
So the first thing I did was write a unit
test to check for that condition.

03:20:30.060 --> 03:20:34.141
Here we see the first example of the process
I used to write these algorithms.

03:20:34.141 --> 03:20:39.040
I tried to solve the problem one step at a
time and to verify the correctness of each

03:20:39.040 --> 03:20:40.420
step as I went.

03:20:40.420 --> 03:20:46.900
Next, I knew that I needed to create the edges
or relationships between different tiles based

03:20:46.900 --> 03:20:52.780
on the rules of the game, nodes would share
a column may not have the same value or color

03:20:52.780 --> 03:20:57.521
nodes would share a row may not have the same
value, nodes would share the same sub grid

03:20:57.521 --> 03:20:58.900
may not have the same value.

03:20:58.900 --> 03:21:03.500
Now, we haven't actually added any values
yet, but we can still build the edges of the

03:21:03.500 --> 03:21:05.640
graph using these rules.

03:21:05.640 --> 03:21:09.810
After looking at a picture of a four by four,
nine by nine in 16.

03:21:09.810 --> 03:21:14.460
By 16 Sudoku puzzle, I was able to determine
that every tile will have the same number

03:21:14.460 --> 03:21:21.521
of edges as any other tile, and those numbers
happened to be 821 and 40, respectively.

03:21:21.521 --> 03:21:23.150
At first, the test was failing.

03:21:23.150 --> 03:21:27.660
But I figured out that a node can be for example,
in the same column and subgrid.

03:21:27.660 --> 03:21:33.021
So I needed in my algorithm to be smarter
about avoiding those kinds of repeats.

03:21:33.021 --> 03:21:35.480
The previous two algorithms were childsplay.

03:21:35.480 --> 03:21:38.021
Whereas now we get into some more difficult
problems.

03:21:38.021 --> 03:21:43.601
The seed colors algorithm caused me huge problems
and took many days to get working properly.

03:21:43.601 --> 03:21:49.230
The purpose of this algorithm was to provide
some initial values, ie seed values to make

03:21:49.230 --> 03:21:52.021
the next step in the algorithm easier.

03:21:52.021 --> 03:21:56.920
At first, I tried to do this by distributing
the values diagonally, which is a relatively

03:21:56.920 --> 03:21:59.771
safe way to avoid breaking the rules.

03:21:59.771 --> 03:22:03.660
But this approach led me to an uneven distribution
of initial values.

03:22:03.660 --> 03:22:07.940
I thought this was a problem and eventually
came up with a new algorithm that would make

03:22:07.940 --> 03:22:14.160
horizontal and vertical passes of the entire
puzzle, allocating numbers in such a way that

03:22:14.160 --> 03:22:19.170
was guaranteed not to break the rules and
provide an even distribution.

03:22:19.170 --> 03:22:23.330
While I'm proud of this algorithm in a certain
way, on a certain level, it's also disgustingly

03:22:23.330 --> 03:22:27.700
complicated and proved pretty difficult to
test, I settled on ensuring that it wasn't

03:22:27.700 --> 03:22:33.600
creating an invalid puzzle, and it was allocating
a number of values roughly equal to A quarter

03:22:33.600 --> 03:22:36.070
of the total number of tiles.

03:22:36.070 --> 03:22:39.970
If I allocated too many tiles, it would cause
problems though, so I had to include some

03:22:39.970 --> 03:22:43.320
hard coded edge cases, which I didn't really
like.

03:22:43.320 --> 03:22:49.240
Perhaps most important algorithm in this series
of algorithms is the solver algorithm.

03:22:49.240 --> 03:22:54.771
The solver algorithm takes in our seated graph
and attempts to solve the puzzle from there.

03:22:54.771 --> 03:23:00.261
Now I had already written a few other solver
algorithms that didn't use graph data structures.

03:23:00.261 --> 03:23:04.600
So I was curious to see if using a graph would
actually make this process easier.

03:23:04.600 --> 03:23:08.980
In some ways it did, but it was still overall
quite difficult to write a high performance

03:23:08.980 --> 03:23:12.690
solver for n sized Sudoku puzzles.

03:23:12.690 --> 03:23:19.090
Every algorithm for generating a Sudoku puzzle
that I'm aware of makes usage of three things.

03:23:19.090 --> 03:23:24.680
brute force random number assignment, checking
if these new assignments create an invalid

03:23:24.680 --> 03:23:26.150
puzzle.

03:23:26.150 --> 03:23:31.720
backtracking when an invalid puzzle is created,
or the algorithm is simply not able to allocate

03:23:31.720 --> 03:23:36.080
any new values without actually breaking the
rules of the game.

03:23:36.080 --> 03:23:40.900
The problem with brute force random number
assignment is that it scales very poorly as

03:23:40.900 --> 03:23:46.210
the size of the puzzle grows, randomly generating
a four by four Sudoku puzzle is quite easy

03:23:46.210 --> 03:23:47.210
that way.

03:23:47.210 --> 03:23:51.590
But even a nine by nine puzzle can take a
long time if you just use random numbers and

03:23:51.590 --> 03:23:53.200
backtracking.

03:23:53.200 --> 03:23:56.740
There's no way the app would be any good if
the user had to sit and wait for five minutes

03:23:56.740 --> 03:23:59.610
just for the app to generate a new puzzle.

03:23:59.610 --> 03:24:04.750
As is often the case, the solution to this
problem came from figuring out the right question

03:24:04.750 --> 03:24:10.900
to ask, How do I tell the computer to make
smart decisions about assigning values instead

03:24:10.900 --> 03:24:13.971
of just purely random decisions.

03:24:13.971 --> 03:24:19.431
In the end, I settled on giving the algorithm
a nice value, which is inspired by nice values

03:24:19.431 --> 03:24:21.780
in CPU scheduling.

03:24:21.780 --> 03:24:26.190
In principle, the algorithm will select an
empty tile and look at the number of possible

03:24:26.190 --> 03:24:31.021
values we can assign to that tile with respect
to the rules of the game.

03:24:31.021 --> 03:24:35.330
This is done by looking at the rest of the
elements in the linked list for that tile,

03:24:35.330 --> 03:24:41.080
and seeing how many of them are already colored
that is given a value in a nine by nine puzzle

03:24:41.080 --> 03:24:47.370
if eight other tiles in the list already have
a unique value than the tile we have currently

03:24:47.370 --> 03:24:51.891
selected, can only have one possible correct
value.

03:24:51.891 --> 03:24:57.130
If seven tiles are already colored, we have
a 50% chance of guessing the correct number

03:24:57.130 --> 03:24:59.240
which is still pretty good odds.

03:24:59.240 --> 03:25:04.311
So the nice value represents how picky the
algorithm is in deciding whether or not to

03:25:04.311 --> 03:25:06.380
assign a value.

03:25:06.380 --> 03:25:11.580
The nice value itself is adjusted constantly
with the basic idea being two things.

03:25:11.580 --> 03:25:16.431
If the algorithm has looked at many elements
and could not find a sufficiently safe guess,

03:25:16.431 --> 03:25:21.641
we increment the nice value, thus allowing
the algorithm to make riskier guesses.

03:25:21.641 --> 03:25:26.030
If the algorithm assigns a value, it becomes
pickier again by decrementing the nice value.

03:25:26.030 --> 03:25:31.170
The other important part of this algorithm
is the multistage backtracking.

03:25:31.170 --> 03:25:35.580
This part was very difficult to figure out,
and it required trying a lot of different

03:25:35.580 --> 03:25:36.580
approaches.

03:25:36.580 --> 03:25:41.940
In order to work smoothly for four, nine and
16 boundary puzzles, I settled on using three

03:25:41.940 --> 03:25:44.120
stages of backtracking.

03:25:44.120 --> 03:25:48.650
In the first stage, when the algorithm gets
stuck, we remove half of the values we have

03:25:48.650 --> 03:25:50.801
allocated to the puzzle.

03:25:50.801 --> 03:25:55.650
In the second stage, we remove all values
we have allocated to the puzzle, but we keep

03:25:55.650 --> 03:25:58.070
the same seated values.

03:25:58.070 --> 03:26:03.410
In the final stage, we remove all values and
generate a new seed then reset the algorithm

03:26:03.410 --> 03:26:04.630
to start from scratch.

03:26:04.630 --> 03:26:10.620
After playing around with both the nice values
and the conditions for each stage of backtracking,

03:26:10.620 --> 03:26:15.950
something magical happened, I made one small
change to the nice value adjustment.

03:26:15.950 --> 03:26:21.530
And suddenly, as you can see from my benchmark
tests, the algorithm was generating 101 nine

03:26:21.530 --> 03:26:27.761
by nine Sudoku puzzles in roughly 400 milliseconds
instead of over four minutes.

03:26:27.761 --> 03:26:29.551
At first, I didn't trust what I was seeing.

03:26:29.551 --> 03:26:33.600
But after removing the change I had just made,
we were right back two minutes instead of

03:26:33.600 --> 03:26:35.130
milliseconds.

03:26:35.130 --> 03:26:40.021
The key takeaway is that every time I made
a small change to the algorithm, I rerun the

03:26:40.021 --> 03:26:42.510
benchmarks to see what changed.

03:26:42.510 --> 03:26:47.660
Tests are absolutely critical when you are
writing new algorithms, as they tell you both

03:26:47.660 --> 03:26:51.550
the correctness and efficiency of your code.

03:26:51.550 --> 03:26:56.480
I was super excited to have a working Sudoku
solver that was crushing my benchmarks, but

03:26:56.480 --> 03:27:00.070
there was still one more algorithmic dragon
to slay.

03:27:00.070 --> 03:27:04.801
As you can guess a solved Sudoku puzzle is
not actually useful to a person looking to

03:27:04.801 --> 03:27:09.501
solve a Sudoku game, which happens to be our
entire user base.

03:27:09.501 --> 03:27:14.030
Having generated a complete and valid puzzle,
the next thing to do was to remove a certain

03:27:14.030 --> 03:27:17.420
amount of values to make the game playable
again.

03:27:17.420 --> 03:27:22.330
At first, I thought the difficulty of a Sudoku
puzzle was largely dependent on how many initial

03:27:22.330 --> 03:27:24.620
clues are given to the user.

03:27:24.620 --> 03:27:30.040
That idea is true at the extremes of a complete
puzzle or an empty puzzle, but not so true

03:27:30.040 --> 03:27:31.650
in the middle.

03:27:31.650 --> 03:27:36.580
What I mean is that you can definitely have
a Sudoku puzzle with 33 given clues that is

03:27:36.580 --> 03:27:42.340
easy to solve without guessing and have a
puzzle with 38 given clues that is impossible

03:27:42.340 --> 03:27:44.660
to solve without guessing.

03:27:44.660 --> 03:27:49.570
So I suddenly realized that I was going to
need to invent some way of establishing the

03:27:49.570 --> 03:27:53.360
difficulty of a Sudoku puzzle in a consistent
way.

03:27:53.360 --> 03:27:57.260
Now, I knew that there must be some way to
do this mathematically.

03:27:57.260 --> 03:28:01.351
But the truth is that mathematics is not a
natural way for me to solve problems.

03:28:01.351 --> 03:28:05.380
I was great at calculus, but only for the
reason that I could visualize the graphs which

03:28:05.380 --> 03:28:07.580
the equations described.

03:28:07.580 --> 03:28:11.690
Instead of trying to model this problem in
mathematical terms, I visualize the things

03:28:11.690 --> 03:28:16.311
that I actually do in order to solve various
difficulties of Sudoku puzzles.

03:28:16.311 --> 03:28:20.771
I then figured out how to tell the computer
to employ these different strategies to attempt

03:28:20.771 --> 03:28:23.950
to solve a puzzle without making any random
guesses.

03:28:23.950 --> 03:28:29.070
This led to creating three kinds of strategies
which dictate the difficulty of a given puzzle.

03:28:29.070 --> 03:28:34.090
In the basic solving strategy, a puzzle can
be solved simply by going through each square

03:28:34.090 --> 03:28:39.021
and asking if there is only one possible value
that can be placed in that square based on

03:28:39.021 --> 03:28:40.391
the rules of the game.

03:28:40.391 --> 03:28:45.710
In the advanced strategy, we still use the
basic strategy, but we also look for a situation

03:28:45.710 --> 03:28:50.801
where a node has two possible values, we then
look for another empty node, which has an

03:28:50.801 --> 03:28:55.680
edge to the original node that also has the
same possible two values.

03:28:55.680 --> 03:28:59.880
Finally, we test assigning both values to
both nodes.

03:28:59.880 --> 03:29:04.851
Three things can occur when this happens if
both result in a valid Sudoku puzzle.

03:29:04.851 --> 03:29:08.720
This doesn't actually help much because it's
possible to make valid assignments that still

03:29:08.720 --> 03:29:12.040
result in an invalid puzzle later on.

03:29:12.040 --> 03:29:16.680
If both result in an invalid puzzle, this
is obviously not a good situation.

03:29:16.680 --> 03:29:21.570
Finally, if one configuration is valid, but
the other isn't, then logically, the valid

03:29:21.570 --> 03:29:24.170
configuration is correct.

03:29:24.170 --> 03:29:28.700
The final solving strategy basically means
that the puzzle cannot be solved in a purely

03:29:28.700 --> 03:29:35.221
logical way, ie you must make guesses to solve
it, or it requires some kind of advanced strategy,

03:29:35.221 --> 03:29:36.900
which I'm not aware of.

03:29:36.900 --> 03:29:40.140
I'm not an expert in Sudoku, but I'm better
than the average player.

03:29:40.140 --> 03:29:42.739
So either way, I would consider that to be
a hard puzzle.

